WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.219
Before you install: a review routine.

2
00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:07.789
Most of the risk in your daily work comes not from servers you write but from servers you add.

3
00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:16.069
The MCP docs’ local-server guide gives a routine that takes a few minutes per server, and it maps directly onto Claude Code.

4
00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:18.333
Ask whether it needs to run locally.

5
00:00:18.613 --> 00:00:23.499
A server that only wraps a remote API gains nothing from running on your laptop.

6
00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:33.080
If a remote version exists, use it: no code runs on your machine, and it uses the protocol’s authorization instead of a token in a config file.

7
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:40.485
A third-party server that needs nothing local yet ships only as a stdio process is worth a second look.

8
00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:41.789
Check provenance.

9
00:00:42.069 --> 00:00:49.813
A registry listing is not an endorsement, and the guide notes that even curated catalogs describe their vetting as best effort.

10
00:00:50.093 --> 00:01:00.525
Tie the package to a source repository and a real maintainer, verify the exact package name (typosquatting is the cheapest attack there is), and pin the version.

11
00:01:00.805 --> 00:01:15.547
Know what the pin covers: pinning a package run through npx or uvx fixes the top-level package, while its dependencies may still resolve fresh at install time; a container image pinned by digest freezes the whole tree.

12
00:01:15.827 --> 00:01:16.872
Read the tools.

13
00:01:17.152 --> 00:01:22.656
Open the server in the Inspector, read every description, and run an audit like the one above.

14
00:01:22.936 --> 00:01:27.053
Remove servers whose descriptions say things unrelated to their job.

15
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:28.208
Contain it.

16
00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:40.627
Run third-party servers in a container with only the directory they need mounted, no network unless they need it, and their own narrowly scoped credentials passed through the host’s per-server environment setting.

17
00:01:40.907 --> 00:01:41.931
Keep watching.

18
00:01:42.211 --> 00:01:49.379
Re-check after updates, remove servers you no longer use, and rotate the credentials a server held when you remove it.

19
00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:52.965
Research tools are emerging to automate parts of this.

20
00:01:53.245 --> 00:02:08.776
Radosevich and Halloran (2025) showed that leading models could be coerced through MCP tools into malicious code execution, remote access, and credential theft, and proposed a scanner that probes a server before deployment.

21
00:02:09.056 --> 00:02:12.384
Treat such tools as one more check, not a certification.
